\subsubsection*{4.2 BST Traversals}

A binary search tree can expose its in-order, pre-order, or post-order
traversal as a generator. \texttt{yield from} recursively delegates to
subtree traversals, so the shape of the tree determines the output
order.

Because the traversal is a generator method (not a stored generator
instance), the tree is re-iterable: two successive \texttt{list(t)}
calls each start a fresh traversal.

A skewed insert order (all values decreasing) produces a tree that is a
single path; the in-order traversal is still sorted, but the pre-order
traversal reveals the degenerate shape.
